ADR-001: Choose a Centralized Architecture for v0.1
Problem

The system must allow employees to create and follow internal service requests, while authorized department staff can receive, manage, and complete requests for their departments.

At this stage, the expected number of employees, daily requests, and overall system traffic are not clearly defined.

Because the future system load is still uncertain, introducing a distributed architecture such as microservices or message queues may add unnecessary complexity.

The architecture should therefore remain simple and easy to modify while the project requirements are still developing.

Options
Option 1: Simple Centralized Architecture

Use one App/Web application that communicates with a single Backend. The Backend is responsible for communicating with persistent storage.

Advantages:

Easy to develop and maintain.
Meets the requirements currently identified.
Requires fewer components.
Easier to understand and troubleshoot.
Allows the system to change easily as requirements evolve.
Option 2: Distributed Architecture

Use several separate components from the beginning, such as microservices, message queues, Redis, or other external services.

Advantages:

Components can be scaled separately.
Better suited for systems with high traffic and complex workloads.
Can support more advanced scalability requirements.

Disadvantages:

More difficult to design and maintain.
Requires additional infrastructure and configuration.
Creates more operational complexity.
The current project requirements do not require this level of architecture.
Decision

For version 0.1, the project will use a simple centralized architecture.

The App/Web application will communicate directly with the Backend, and the Backend will communicate with the persistent database.

No additional distributed components will be introduced at this stage.

If the number of users, requests, or system requirements increases significantly in the future, the architecture can be changed accordingly.

Consequences
Positive
The system remains simple during the initial development stage.
Development and testing are easier.
Maintenance requires less effort.
There are fewer components and dependencies to manage.
The architecture is sufficient for the currently known requirements.
Future changes can be introduced without unnecessary complexity at the beginning.
Negative
The system does not provide the same independent scaling capabilities as a distributed architecture.
A single Backend may become a limitation if the system grows considerably.
Additional architectural changes may be required if traffic becomes much higher than expected.
Future Consideration

When reliable information becomes available about the number of users, request volume, traffic, performance, and scalability requirements, this architectural decision should be reviewed.

If the system grows beyond what the centralized architecture can comfortably support, components such as caching, message queues, or separate services can be introduced when they provide a clear benefit.
